  note: §4.19: a record lent read-only crosses as a pointer to `const` of its own struct, and a `void *` drops both the struct and the `const`: write a header of your own that declares a `static inline` function taking `p` as that pointer and handing it on to `pt_poke_any`, and bind that one; or, where C may write it, take it `@p: <record> counted_by <count>`

error[ffi_writable_parameter]: `p` of `pt_reset` is lent read-only as a `Pt`, and the header says `struct pt *` — C does not promise to leave it alone
  at tests/golden/unsupported/ffi-a-record-lent-read-only-to-a-header-that-may-write-it.hero:17:23
     |
  17 |     function pt_reset(p: Pt lent) -> i32  #~ ffi_writable_parameter
     |                       ^
  note: `lent` with no `@` hands C the caller's own place to read: a C write through it would change a value the program never handed over to change
  fix (guess): where C changes it, take it `@p` and lend it at each call with `@`
